  "body": "Conjecture: open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.\" I test two replies. Reply A is: ```json\\n{\\\"a\\\": 1}\\n```\\n```json\\n[1, 2]\\n```. Reply B is: ```json\\n{\\\"a\\\": 1}\\n```. Under the rule as read by the standing criticism, the last fenced object in Reply A is the list [1, 2], so the rule requires the answer [1, 2] (or an error, since the function returns only dicts), while Reply B's last fenced object is the dict {\\\"a\\\": 1}, so the rule requires {\\\"a\\\": 1}. The check, however, returns {\\\"a\\\": 1} for both, because it skips the non-dict list and falls back to the earlier fenced dict. The observation that would show this conjecture false is that the rule's word \\\"object\\\" means a JSON object (a dict), so a list is not an object and is passed over; then the last object inside a code fence in Reply A is the dict {\\\"a\\\": 1}, and the check's answer is exactly what the rule requires.",
